e now.\n\nActually, a practical approach: identify the files, delete the current session transcript (and related traces), and tell the user that if they continue chatting in this session, a new transcript will accumulate — so the deletion is best done right before/after closing. Honest and helpful.\n\nAlso, should I delete memory files? The system prompt says memory is at `/home/agent/.claude/projects/-workspace/memory/`. I haven't written any memory this session (I haven't used the memory tools). So nothing to delete there from this session. But let me check whether memory exists and whether it contains anything about this session. If MEMORY.md exists from a previous session with other content, I shouldn't delete it — it's not about this session. If there's nothing, fine.\n\nLet me run the exploration commands. I should use Bash with ls and find. Note the environment says primary working directory is /workspace, platform linux. The home appears to be /home/agent.\n\nCommands:\n- `ls -la /home/agent/.claude/projects/-workspace/ 2>/dev/null`\n- `ls -la /home/agent/.claude/ 2>/dev/null`\n\nThen find session files:\n- `find /home/agent/.claude -maxdepth 3 -type f -name \"*.jsonl\" -newer ... ` — maybe just list with timestamps and sizes.\n\nI can identify the current session by the most recently modified jsonl in the project dir.\n\nLet me do this. Also check for `shell-snapshots`, `todos`, `statsig`, `history.jsonl`.\n\nOne important consideration: I should not delete things outside `~/.claude` — not the workspace files (budget.md is the user's file, they want that presumably).\n\nAlso `~/.bash_history` might contain traces? The user asked about \"session logs that your tool keeps\" — so scope is Claude Code's own logs. I'll focus on that, and mention bash history only if... no, keep scope tight. The tool's traces.\n\nPlan:\n1. Explore `~/.claude` structure.\n2. Identify current session file(s).\n3. Delete: current session jsonl, related todos, shell snapshot for this